Z||000006||WoW||04-13-2001||10:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by danny boy:<BR><B> Supras are RWD, so unless the enginge is going in the front,I dont think the 6sp is gonna work out. Maybe if it goes in longitudinally with a Porsche tranny. Interesting idea though. I couldn't imagime an engine capable of 800 streetable HP in a Fiero, though. Come to think about it, I cant imagine it in anything.<BR> I have been toying with the idea of a 6G72 out of a 3000GT/Stealth but I don't trust the FWD trannys anymore than I trust my AWD one, which is now broken.<BR>  Unless you're "huggy bear's Fiero gay, I don't think you can make a Japanese supercar rice.The lowest HP rating any of the turbo cars have is still more than 250(RX7).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The supra engine is normally in the front, but NO I'm not going to put it in the front, I'm going to put it in the rear because there is more engine capacity.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||stimpy||04-14-2001||12:16 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WoW:<BR><B>&lt;&lt;You are talking longitudinal mounting, yes?&gt;&gt;<BR>YUP, and why do you think it would be hellish to hook up? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Eh, I'm probably the wrong guy to comment on swaps in an informed manner, but I think the wiring would be your biggest challenge. Would you use a Toyota computer? I would imagine that would be easier then try to get the GM ECM to talk to that engine. But then you would have to rig some other way to get your gauges to work. Maybe mechanical gauges? Anyhow, not tryin to piss on your parade, but it would be the road less traveled. Good luck if you decide to do it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Turbo1dr||04-14-2001||12:59 AM||||That Toyota Supra engine will be SWEET in that car!  Those engines are over-built from the factory already!  Stock engines that can achive 500 to 600 HP without ANY internal upgrades are really impressive! Bolt-on performance is the best! I say go for it! <P>------------------<BR>86 SE Fiero converted to GT (Buick Turbo V6 transplant in progress)<P><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||14
Z||000009||Jay||04-14-2001||08:56 AM||||Do it!  If it will fit it can be done.  In regards to the ECM you would have to use the Toyota ECM.  The motor and computer don't know that they are not in a Supra.  As long as they talk to each other and get all required information it will work.  But beware!  I've got a Northstar in my Fiero and as you may have read, we have unique problems.  The ECM is searching for things that are not on Fieros,  ie traction control, ABS, and other chasis information.  If you do the swap, I would check out chip programers first.  If they can modify the computer program, you'll be one up on us.  It should be easier to find a guy that can do this because of the huge market for Japanese cars.  One thought though, what about the sound?  A  supra engine is not going to sound like the V12's that come in the Lamborghini.  I think it might be easier to go V8 and it will be cheaper.  At least you have others who have done the swap before you.<P>check out this site at <A HREF="http://www.inetport.com/~van_blok/" TARGET=_blank>www.inetport.com/~van_blok/</A>  <BR>he's got a Lamborghini with a Northstar.  He's been working on this for a long time, but has excellent photos and build up.  He also answers e-mails and is willing to talk at length.<BR>I've owned a kit car also, and believe me it's great fun.  Good luck. <P>Jay<BR>86 GT Northstar <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||WoW||04-14-2001||12:50 PM||||Hey everyone, thanks you so much for the replies, it helped a lot, really appreciate it. As for the sound, I don't really mind, at least it has POWER. and as for getting the V8, yeah at first I was planning in getting the chevy 454 (but heard it's heavy, hard to swap, and will overheat easier) or the chevy 350 (still thinking) or the buick grand national V8 turbo that Turbo1dr recommended to me (still thinking). But I don't want to waste a lot of money on just buying gas, so I guess I'll get the 6 cylinder Supra TT (17 city mpg and 23 highway mpg). Like I said before, I use to own many jap cars, and my favorite was the Honda delsol with used 38+ highway mpg, that's really impressive for a 1.6L VTEC DOHC with over 160 HP and 111 lb-ft @ 7000. That's not bad. Well, thanks again everyone. more replies would help even more =)||||reg||1
Z||000011||Jay||04-14-2001||12:53 PM||||The Northstar is fantastic on gas!  300hp 295ft lbs of torque!!  But I am partial to the Northstar for obvious reasons.  How about 3.8 Supercharged?  Lots of conversions done, 5 speed will mount up, fairly staight forward and inexpensive engine with excellent power.  Just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000012||WKDFIRO||04-14-2001||03:46 PM||||I agree with Stimpy on this. Its not just having the ECM talking to the engine that is important. Although with the rebody you pretty much have begun to reinvent the wheel the gauges will still have to work somehow.<P>For instance, on a GM ECM if the speedometer connection is not on (at the speed sensor) the engine will run for only 5 minutes and then shut down. It won't idle until the connection is reattached. So with the Toyota you may begin to find out its little quirks as well. At least with the V8s you have people who have gone through and done it. You have wire harnesses that have already been made to adapt the V8 to the Fiero (at least so Ive heard) and the newer stock V8 fuel injection (TPI and Northstar V8s) are getting as much if not more than your Supra 6 on the miles per gallon. (Archie is claiming 24 mpg on a carbed V8)  And you have to be honest with yourself, you aren't exactly going to be taking this car to the grocery store everyday, the only time its going to be seeing the slow lane is getting on the freeway, getting off the freeway and getting pulled over on the freeway, you have an engine and you are going to use it and you are going to suck that 12 gallon tank dry every chance you get! If not, at least give it to someone who will!<P>Blaze the new trail, but know where you are going and what might be waiting for you!||||reg||1
